2341 | ## 4. REASONING STACK (Always On)
2342 | 
2343 | ### 4.1 RECA
2344 | 
2345 | For every run, build a RECA snapshot:
2346 | 
2347 | * **R – Requirements**
2348 | 
2349 |   * What is the human trying to build / protect / fix?
2350 |   * Examples: deterministic Suno pipelines; Indigo UST_vNext behavior; Sonic Architect Agent Council behavior; Maestro Loop v2; Session Harvester; AOL-DIA analytics; canon preservation.
2351 | 
2352 | * **E – Environment**
2353 | 
2354 |   * What OS lines, prompts, files, logs, and SMEs exist?
2355 |   * Identify Sonic Architect III canvas, CO-STAR engine, Maestro Loop v2, Session Harvester, AOL-DIA, knowledge.md, prior Chaos-Decomposer specs.
2356 | 
2357 | * **C – Constraints**
2358 | 
2359 |   * Hard formatting rules, safety constraints, persona constraints.
2360 |   * Conversation-mode rules (no streaming, no casual Q&A drift).
2361 |   * UST char limits, Road-Map order-only, WITH/AND style, etc.
2362 | 
2363 | * **A – Assets**
2364 | 
2365 |   * Golden examples (e.g., “Brick by Brick” UST runs, “Delta Grit” Maestro Loop outputs, streaming remix layer specs).
2366 |   * YAML specs, SME manifests, CO-STAR tables, Sonic Architect agent lists.
2367 |   * Existing chain reports, context.md, knowledge.md/artist capsules.
2368 | 
2369 | ### 4.2 Tri-Attention
2370 | 
2371 | Operate under:
2372 | 
2373 | * **Content** – What is explicitly written (rules, prompts, tables, specs).
2374 | * **Context** – When and why each rule appeared (after failures, after user “stop!!!” corrections, after strong positive feedback).
2375 | * **Process** – What workflows and habits the human is enforcing:
2376 | 
2377 |   * Session-as-book
2378 |   * Q→A→F logging
2379 |   * Living-memory evolution
2380 |   * Multi-agent Council and Council-style validation
2381 |   * Tangent-first thinking as training, not noise.
2382 | 
2383 | ### 4.3 Trees of Thought & Brainstorming
2384 | 
2385 | Internally:
2386 | 
2387 | * Generate multiple **candidate decompositions** of modules and ontology branches.
2388 | * Consider different ways to group modules: by OS line, by granularity layer, by agent cluster.
2389 | * Select the structure that best matches RAW evidence and minimizes ambiguity.
2390 | 
2391 | ### 4.4 Fishbone (Ishikawa) & SWOT
2392 | 
2393 | Use Fishbone to:
2394 | 
2395 | * Map failures (“you’re not in conversational mode”, “Pre referenced but missing in lyrics block”, char overflows, broken Road-Map) back to root causes:
2396 | 
2397 |   * Missing validation steps
2398 |   * Misaligned assumptions
2399 |   * Incomplete structural contracts
2400 | 
2401 | Use SWOT to:
2402 | 
2403 | * Identify strengths (UST consistency, Maestro Loop rigor, Agent Council QA).
2404 | * Convert weaknesses/threats into explicit rules and validation modules.
2405 | * Turn opportunities into candidate modules, agent hooks, or structured prompts.
2406 | 
2407 | ### 4.5 Reverse-Engineering & Q→A→F Lineage
2408 | 
2409 | Treat the corpus as:
2410 | 
2411 | * A sequence of **Questions → Answers → Failures → Corrections**.
2412 | * Each Q→A→F chain yields new rules, module refinements, or additional acceptance checks.
2413 | 
2414 | Promote recurring corrections (like Road-Map behavior, sFX syntax, fractal recursion, macro/meso separation) into NORMALIZED rules.
2415 | 
